consiSt("wi",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"Wisconsin Supreme Court","d":"Has appellate jurisdiction only, except as the constitution or law provides (it also hears original actions and supervisory writs under ss. 809.70–809.71). Reviews Court of Appeals decisions only by discretionary petition for review (s. 808.10; s. 809.62), and can take a case directly by bypass petition, certification from the Court of Appeals, or on its own motion (s. 808.05). Writes the procedure statutes by rule (s. 751.12) and controls bar admission (SCR ch. 40), the State Bar and pro hac vice (SCR ch. 10), and lawyer ethics (SCR ch. 20)."},{"l":"INTERMEDIATE","n":"Wisconsin Court of Appeals","d":"16 judges in four districts: District I (Milwaukee), II (Waukesha), III (Wausau), IV (Madison) (ss. 752.03, 752.11–752.19). Hears appeals of right from final circuit court judgments and orders, appeals by permission from nonfinal orders, and supervisory and prerogative writs (ss. 752.01–752.02, 808.03). Sits in 3-judge panels, but small claims (ch. 799), ordinance and traffic, ch. 48/938 juvenile, ch. 51/55, misdemeanor, forfeiture and contempt cases are decided by one judge unless a 3-judge panel is granted (s. 752.31)."},{"l":"TRIAL","n":"Circuit Courts","d":"The single general-jurisdiction trial court, organized by county judicial circuits with numbered branches and grouped into judicial administrative districts (ss. 753.03, 753.06, 753.061). Civil procedure is statutory: Wis. Stat. chs. 801–847, written largely by Supreme Court rule (s. 801.01(2)). Circuits may adopt local rules approved by the chief judge of the administrative district (s. 753.35). Small claims are handled in circuit court under ch. 799 (not collected here)."},{"l":"OTHER","n":"Municipal courts","d":"Local courts for ordinance violations; their judgments can be transcribed to circuit court (s. 806.12). Not otherwise covered here."}],"slapp":[{"t":"No anti-SLAPP statute in force","d":"Wisconsin has no anti-SLAPP law. The statutes certified November 4, 2026 contain no special motion to dismiss for speech or petitioning (ch. 802 has no s. 802.065, and s. 895.69 is the structured-settlement transfer procedure, not an anti-SLAPP provision).\nThe 2025–26 Legislature considered the Uniform Public Expression Protection Act (2025 A.B. 701 / S.B. 666), which would have created s. 802.065 and added it to the motion defenses in s. 802.06(2). A.B. 701 passed the Assembly on February 17, 2026, but both bills died in the Senate on March 23, 2026 under Senate Joint Resolution 1. Earlier 2023 bills also were not enacted.\nWhat a speech defendant can use instead: a motion to dismiss for failure to state a claim, which automatically stays discovery for up to 180 days (s. 802.06(1)(b)); the libel retraction-notice rule (s. 895.05(2)); sanctions under s. 802.05; and frivolous-claim fees under s. 895.044. None of these is a substitute for fee-shifting anti-SLAPP protection.","c":"Wis. Stat. chs. 802, 895 (2023-24, updated 11-4-26); 2025 A.B. 701 and S.B. 666 histories","v":true},{"t":"Frivolous-claim fees (s. 895.044) are the closest fee tool","d":"A party or the party’s lawyer can be liable for actual costs and reasonable attorney fees for commencing, using, or continuing a claim, defense, or appeal that was brought in bad faith solely to harass or maliciously injure, or that the party or lawyer knew or should have known had no reasonable basis in law or equity. The court must find this by clear and convincing evidence. There is a 21-day safe harbor: if the challenged claim is withdrawn or corrected within 21 days after service of the motion, an award is discretionary; if not, the award is mandatory.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 895.044(1)-(2)","v":true}],"oos":[{"t":"Pro hac vice: court motion with a Wisconsin sponsor, after applying to the State Bar","d":"A circuit court or judge may let nonresident counsel appear in a particular action in association with an active member of the State Bar of Wisconsin who also appears and participates (SCR 10.03(4)(b)). The applicant must give the information in Appendix A to SCR 10 (sworn statement: admissions, no pending discipline, no suspension or disbarment, name and bar number of the Wisconsin sponsor, does not practice in Wisconsin, number of prior Wisconsin PHV applications this calendar year).\nOrder of steps (court guidance): apply and pay online at wisbar.org/prohacvice first; then the Wisconsin sponsor files the motion with the court, attaching the application and proof of payment, plus anything local rules require. The court decides; the State Bar and OLR do not.\nThe sponsoring lawyer must actually participate; how much is up to each court. A lawyer registered only as in-house counsel under SCR 10.03(4)(f) cannot sponsor.","c":"SCR 10.03(4)(b), (b)1, App. A-1; wicourts.gov Pro hac vice FAQ","v":true},{"t":"Fee: $250 per application, nonrefundable, paid to the State Bar","d":"$250 per application, per case (consolidated or jointly litigated cases can share one application). Not refunded if the case is dismissed or the application is withdrawn or denied. Waived for lawyers representing an indigent client through an indigent-services agency or without charge. Nonresident military counsel representing military personnel and counsel representing a tribe in an ICWA custody case need no Wisconsin sponsor and pay no fee (SCR 10.03(4)(c), (cm)). No fee to move a bar applicant’s admission at a Supreme Court ceremony.\nThe court says the fee is split $50 to OLR, $100 to the Wisconsin Trust Account Foundation, and $100 to the Access to Justice Commission.\nWatch: the Appendix A-1 form in SCR 10 still refers to paying the fee to the Office of Lawyer Regulation; the rule’s comment and the court’s FAQ say it goes to the State Bar (since July 1, 2019).","c":"SCR 10.03(4)(b)2 & Wis. Comment; SCR 10.03(4)(c), (cm); wicourts.gov Pro hac vice FAQ","v":true},{"t":"One admission follows the case up and down","d":"An order granting PHV continues through later appellate or circuit court proceedings in the same matter, provided counsel files notice of the order with the court handling the later proceeding. Make sure the order is in the record on appeal. A court may rescind PHV after a hearing for incompetence or unwillingness to follow the ethics rules or court decorum.","c":"SCR 10.03(4)(b), (e)","v":true},{"t":"Agency hearings, depositions, and temporary practice","d":"An administrative law judge may let nonresident counsel appear in a particular agency proceeding without local counsel, using the same standards as a court (SCR 10.03(4)(d)); the court says no State Bar application or fee is needed for agency matters. For depositions and other work with no court appearance, look to SCR 20:5.5(c), which allows occasional services by a lawyer admitted and in good standing elsewhere (with active local co-counsel, or related to a proceeding or ADR in which the lawyer is or expects to be authorized, or arising out of the home practice). A lawyer practicing under 5.5(c) or (d) must consent to the Clerk of the Wisconsin Supreme Court as agent for service of process (SCR 20:5.5(e)).","c":"SCR 10.03(4)(d); SCR 20:5.5(c)-(e); wicourts.gov Pro hac vice FAQ","v":true},{"t":"In-house counsel must register within 60 days","d":"A lawyer admitted elsewhere who works in Wisconsin on a continuing basis exclusively for a non-law-firm employer must register as in-house counsel with the Board of Bar Examiners within 60 days after starting work, with the Appendix B application, a $250 fee, proof of admission, and an employer affidavit. Registration allows advice to the employer and its affiliates and pro bono work, but not court appearances; appearing in court still requires PHV.","c":"SCR 10.03(4)(f) & Wis. Comment; SCR 20:5.5(d)(1)","v":true},{"t":"Full admission without the bar exam","d":"Wisconsin admits on proof of practice: 3 years of substantial practice within the last 5 years, with admission by another state’s court of last resort ($850 application fee). An applicant who failed the Wisconsin bar exam cannot use this route. Wisconsin also keeps the diploma privilege for graduates of ABA-approved Wisconsin law schools. From July 2026, Wisconsin gives the UBE and accepts a transferred UBE score of 260 or higher if applied for within 36 months, plus a 21-hour Wisconsin law component.","c":"SCR 40.03, 40.04(2), (9)-(10), 40.05(1), (4), 40.14(3)","v":true},{"t":"E-filing: PHV lawyers must register for eFiling after the PHV order","d":"Licensed Wisconsin lawyers and lawyers appearing under SCR 10.03(4) are mandatory e-filing users in circuit court and the appellate courts. A PHV lawyer registers after the court approves the PHV motion, then must ‘opt in’ on each case; a mandatory user who does not opt in gets no notices or electronic service. The court’s FAQ says all PHV attorneys must create an eCourts account.","c":"Wis. Stat. §§ 801.18(3)(a), (f)-(g), 809.801(3); wicourts.gov Pro hac vice FAQ","v":true},{"t":"Limited-scope appearances are allowed by statute","d":"A lawyer may appear for only specific proceedings or issues by filing and serving a notice of limited appearance before or at the proceeding. The notice must name the client, list the covered proceedings or issues, state that the lawyer will file a notice of termination, and give the client’s contact information. Both the client and the lawyer must be served while it lasts. The role ends, without a court order, when the lawyer files and serves a notice of termination stating all covered services and court-ordered acts are complete.\nGhostwriting is allowed but disclosed: a document drafted for a self-represented person must say, next to the signature, ‘This document was prepared with the assistance of a lawyer.’","c":"Wis. Stat. §§ 802.045, 801.14(2m), 802.05(2m); SCR 20:1.2(c), (cm)","v":true}],"ethics":[{"t":"Governing code: SCR chapter 20 (Wisconsin Rules of Professional Conduct)","d":"Based on the ABA Model Rules, with Wisconsin Committee Comments that flag every departure. Adopted June 10, 1987 (effective January 1, 1988), most recently amended March 30, 2023 per the chapter history. ABA comments are printed but the numbering often differs, so check the Wisconsin text first.","c":"SCR ch. 20 (history note)","v":true},{"t":"Mandatory disclosure to prevent client crime or fraud (20:1.6(b))","d":"Unlike Model Rule 1.6, Wisconsin requires (‘shall reveal’) disclosure to the extent reasonably necessary to prevent a client’s criminal or fraudulent act likely to cause death, substantial bodily harm, or substantial injury to another’s financial interests or property. Permissive disclosure in 20:1.6(c)(1) uses ‘reasonably likely’ rather than ‘reasonably certain.’ The conflict-check exception in (c)(6) is broader than Model Rule 1.6(b)(7).","c":"SCR 20:1.6(b)-(c) & Wis. Comm. Comments","v":true},{"t":"Fee terms in writing when the total will exceed $1,000","d":"Scope and fee basis must be communicated in writing unless total cost is foreseeably $1,000 or less, and any change in rate must be in writing. Above $1,000, the purpose and effect of any retainer or advance fee must be in writing. Contingent fees need a writing signed by the client; none in family matters (except collecting past-due support, maintenance, or property division) or criminal defense. Fee division by referral requires the client’s signed written consent. Advanced fees go in trust unless the lawyer uses the SCR 20:1.5(g) alternative.","c":"SCR 20:1.5(b)-(h) & Wis. Comm. Comment","v":true},{"t":"Conflict waivers must be signed by the client","d":"Informed consent to a current-client conflict must be confirmed in a writing signed by the client, not just ‘confirmed in writing’ as in the Model Rule.","c":"SCR 20:1.7(b) Wis. Comm. Comment","v":true},{"t":"Lateral screening is narrow","d":"A former-client conflict of a lateral lawyer is not imputed only if the lawyer did no more than minor and isolated work on the matter at the old firm, is timely screened and gets no part of the fee, and the former client gets prompt written notice (plus a public-defender variant). This is narrower than Model Rule 1.10(a)(2), which allows screening regardless of how much the lateral worked on the matter.","c":"SCR 20:1.10(a)(2)-(3)","v":true},{"t":"Other Wisconsin-specific rules","d":"Insurance defense: the insurer may limit the scope of representation of the insured (20:1.2(e)); 20:1.8(f) keeps an insurance-defense exception. Lawyers must tell unrepresented persons their role (20:4.3). Inadvertently sent privileged material: stop reviewing, notify, and follow instructions pending a court ruling (20:4.4(c)). A lawyer may advise or supervise lawful investigations involving deception (20:4.1(b)). Ex parte scheduling contact with a judge requires prompt notice to the other side (20:3.5(b)). Misconduct includes violating supreme court rules or orders, the attorney’s oath, failing to cooperate with OLR, and harassment based on listed traits (20:8.4(f)-(i)). Rule 3.1 uses a subjective test.","c":"SCR 20:1.2(e), 1.8(f), 3.1, 3.5(b), 4.1(b), 4.3, 4.4(c), 8.4(f)-(i) & Wis. Comm. Comments","v":true}],"style":"State of Wisconsin","home":"https://www.wicourts.gov/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"Filing commences the case, but you have 90 days to serve, and that cannot be extended","d":"An action is commenced when the summons and complaint are filed, but only if an authenticated copy is served within 90 days after filing. The court cannot enlarge the 90 days. Service must be made with reasonable diligence; substitute service at the home and publication are allowed only after reasonable diligence fails at personal service.","c":"Wis. Stat. §§ 801.02(1), 801.15(2)(a), 801.11(1)"},{"t":"Answer deadline is 20 days, or 45 days in tort, insurer, and state cases","d":"An answer is due 20 days after service of the complaint, but 45 days if any claim sounds in tort, if an insurance company is a defendant, or if the defendant is the state. The summons must state the correct period (20 or 45 days; 40 days after first publication if served by publication). The same 45-day rule applies to replies, motions to strike, and responses to amended pleadings.","c":"Wis. Stat. §§ 801.09(2), 802.06(1), (6), 802.09(1)"},{"t":"Do not plead a dollar amount in a tort complaint","d":"In a tort claim for money, the demand for judgment may not state the amount sought. You must disclose the amount to the court and other parties before a hearing on summary judgment or judgment on the pleadings, and before default or final judgment.","c":"Wis. Stat. §§ 802.02(1m), 802.06(3), 802.08(2), 806.01(1)(c), 806.02(2)"},{"t":"Time computation: periods under 11 days skip weekends and holidays","d":"Exclude Saturdays, Sundays, and holidays (including a full day on Good Friday) when a period is less than 11 days. Add 3 days for mail service. Add 1 day for fax, email, or e-filing service only if completed between 5 p.m. and midnight. Late motions to enlarge need excusable neglect, and the court cannot extend the time to appeal, to decide post-verdict motions, or to move under s. 805.17(3) or s. 806.07.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 801.15(1)-(2), (5)"},{"t":"60-day window to substitute the judge","d":"The peremptory substitution request must be filed within 60 days (plaintiff: from filing; others: from service) and before any preliminary contested matter is heard. Arguing a contested motion first forfeits it.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 801.58(1)"},{"t":"Summary judgment within 8 months, and amend once within 6 months","d":"Absent a scheduling order, a summary judgment motion must be made within 8 months after the summons and complaint are filed, served at least 20 days before the hearing, with opposing affidavits at least 5 days before. A pleading may be amended once as of course only within 6 months after filing, and a third-party complaint may be served without leave only within 6 months.","c":"Wis. Stat. §§ 802.08(1)-(2), 802.09(1), 803.05(1)"},{"t":"Discovery has hard caps and no initial disclosures","d":"Limits absent stipulation or order: 25 interrogatories including subparts; 10 depositions of no more than 7 hours each; document requests reaching back no more than 5 years before the claim accrued. There is no general initial-disclosure rule, but litigation-funding agreements must be produced unprompted. A motion to dismiss for failure to state a claim, for judgment on the pleadings, or for a more definite statement automatically stays discovery for up to 180 days.","c":"Wis. Stat. §§ 804.08(1)(am), 804.045, 804.09(1), 804.01(2)(bg), 802.06(1)(b)"},{"t":"Jury: demand early, 5/6 verdict, special verdicts","d":"Demand a jury at or before the scheduling or pretrial conference, whichever is first, and pay the fee on the scheduling order’s timetable, or the jury is waived. Five-sixths of the jurors decide, and the same five-sixths must agree on every question needed for a verdict on a claim. Special verdicts are the default. Failing to object at the instruction and verdict conference waives instruction and verdict errors.","c":"Wis. Stat. §§ 805.01(2)-(3), 802.10(3)(e), 805.09(2), 805.12(1), 805.13(3)"},{"t":"Post-verdict motions: 20 days, then deemed denied at 90","d":"Motions after verdict must be filed and served within 20 days after the verdict unless the court sets a longer time within those 20 days. If not decided within 90 days after the verdict, they are deemed denied and judgment is entered. In bench trials, a motion to amend findings or the judgment is due 20 days after entry of judgment; undecided at 90 days, it is deemed denied and the appeal clock starts 90 days after entry.","c":"Wis. Stat. §§ 805.16(1)-(3), 805.17(3)"},{"t":"Appeal deadline depends on notice of entry: 45 or 90 days, unextendable","d":"Appeal within 45 days of entry of the final judgment or order if written notice of entry is given within 21 days of entry; otherwise within 90 days. No court can enlarge it. Serving notice of entry within 21 days is how the winner shortens the loser’s time. Nonfinal orders require a petition for leave within 14 days.","c":"Wis. Stat. §§ 808.04(1), 806.06(5), 809.82(2)(b), 809.50(1)"},{"t":"Email service needs written consent unless both sides e-file","d":"Service of papers by email is allowed only if the recipient consented in writing; between registered e-filing users, the system’s notice of activity is service. Self-represented ‘paper parties’ must be served by traditional methods. Documents needing personal service still need it.","c":"Wis. Stat. §§ 801.14(2), 801.18(6)(a)-(c)"},{"t":"Rule 11 safe harbor and a separate frivolous-claims statute","d":"Sanctions under s. 802.05 have a 21-day safe harbor before filing the motion. Separately, s. 895.044 allows actual fees, on clear and convincing proof, for claims or defenses brought in bad faith or without reasonable basis, also with a 21-day withdrawal window.","c":"Wis. Stat. §§ 802.05(3)(a)1., 895.044(2)"}],"judge":[{"t":"One peremptory substitution of judge per side, with no reason required","d":"Any party may file a written request to substitute a new judge. Deadlines: before the hearing of any preliminary contested matter, and no later than 60 days after the summons and complaint are filed (plaintiff) or 60 days after that party was served (other parties). If a new judge is assigned for trial: 10 days after notice of assignment; 24 hours if notice comes less than 10 days before trial. After a reversal or new-trial remand, a fresh request is allowed within 20 days after the remittitur is filed.\nOne request per party per action, naming one judge; parties united in interest and pleading together count as one. If timely and in proper form, the named judge loses jurisdiction and a new judge is assigned. A ruling that the request is untimely can be reviewed by the chief judge of the administrative district if requested within 10 days. For a judge assigned under s. 751.03, the request is due 7 days after notice of the assignment.","c":"Wis. Stat. §§ 801.58(1)-(3), (7), 801.59","v":true},{"t":"Mandatory disqualification and recusal","d":"A judge must disqualify for kinship to a party or counsel within the 3rd degree, being a party or material witness, prior work as counsel in the same case or on a disputed instrument, a significant financial or personal interest, or when the judge determines that the judge cannot, or it appears the judge cannot, act impartially. All parties and the judge may waive after full disclosure on the record. Abuse is referred to the Judicial Commission.\nThe Code of Judicial Conduct adds an objective standard: recusal when reasonable, well-informed persons would reasonably question impartiality. A lawful campaign contribution, an endorsement, or independent expenditures by a party do not by themselves require recusal.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 757.19; SCR 60.04(4), (7), (8)","v":true},{"t":"Judge disqualified by interest: change of venue","d":"When the judge is a party, interested, related to a party, or was counsel for a party, the court must change the place of trial or call in another judge on any party’s application.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 801.56","v":true}],"speech":[{"t":"Libel in a periodical: written retraction demand before suit","d":"Before suing for libel published in a newspaper, magazine, or periodical, the plaintiff must give written notice identifying the article, the statements claimed to be false and defamatory, and the true facts, and allow a reasonable chance to correct. A timely correction published without comment and as prominently as the original limits recovery to actual damages. Also: a newspaper is not liable for a true and fair report of official proceedings (headlines and added comment excepted).","c":"Wis. Stat. § 895.05(1)-(2)","v":true},{"t":"Defamation pleading: quote the words","d":"A libel or slander complaint must set out the particular words complained of; publication and application to the plaintiff may be pleaded generally.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 802.03(6)","v":true},{"t":"Constitutional speech protections broader than federal: not established here","d":"Wisconsin Constitution art. I, §§ 3–4 protect speech, press, assembly, and petition. Whether Wisconsin courts read them more broadly than the First Amendment was not checked; the constitution and case law were not collected.","c":"Wis. Const. art. I, §§ 3–4 (not in collected texts)","v":false}],"other":[{"t":"Procedure is statutory, not a separate rule book","d":"Wisconsin’s rules of civil and appellate procedure are statutes (chs. 801–847 and ch. 809), most created or amended by Supreme Court order under s. 751.12. Cite them as ‘Wis. Stat. § 802.06,’ not as rules. Appellate rules carry the label ‘Rule’ in their titles (e.g., s. 809.19 Rule (Briefs and appendix)).","c":"Wis. Stat. §§ 751.12, 801.01(2)","v":true},{"t":"Unpublished Court of Appeals opinions","d":"An unpublished opinion is never precedent. It may be cited for persuasive value only if issued on or after July 1, 2009 and authored by a judge (not per curiam, memorandum, or summary orders), and a copy must be filed with the brief. Otherwise it may be cited only for claim or issue preclusion or law of the case.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 809.23(3)","v":true},{"t":"Litigation-funding agreements must be disclosed without a request","d":"A party must, without awaiting a discovery request, give the other parties any agreement under which someone other than the party’s contingent-fee lawyer has a right to compensation contingent on and sourced from the proceeds of the case.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 804.01(2)(bg)","v":true}]},"appeals":{"asof":"November 10, 2026","src":"Wis. Stat. ch. 808 (Appeals and Writs of Error) and ch. 809 (Rules of Appellate Procedure), with ss. 801.15, 805.16, 805.17, 806.06, 806.07, 806.08 and 803.08(11), from the 2023-24 Wisconsin Statutes updated through 2025 Wis. Act 247 and all Supreme Court orders in effect November 4, 2026 (docs.legis.wisconsin.gov); Wis. Stat. ch. 752 (Court of Appeals). Summaries are Consi’s.","intro":{"t":"Appeals in Wisconsin: the clock runs from entry of the final judgment, and nothing extends it","d":"Civil appeals go from the circuit court to the Court of Appeals as of right from a final judgment or final order (one that disposes of the entire matter as to one or more parties), or by permission from a nonfinal order. The Supreme Court reviews the Court of Appeals only by discretionary petition for review, or takes a case early by bypass or certification.\nThe notice of appeal is filed in the circuit court within 45 days of entry of the final judgment if written notice of entry was given within 21 days, otherwise within 90 days. A timely notice is jurisdictional, and no court can enlarge the time. Wisconsin has no federal-style tolling rule: post-verdict and reconsideration motions are decided before judgment is entered, are deemed denied after 90 days, and the appeal runs from entry of the final judgment or order.\nAll procedure is statutory (ch. 809 rules are statutes adopted by Supreme Court order). Lawyers must e-file in both appellate courts.","c":"Wis. Stat. §§ 808.03, 808.04(1), 808.05, 808.10, 809.10(1)(e), 809.82(2)(b)"},"groups":[["What can be appealed",[{"t":"Appeal as of right: final judgments and final orders","d":"A final judgment or order is one that disposes of the entire matter in litigation as to one or more parties and is entered under s. 806.06(1)(b) (filed with the clerk) or s. 807.11(2). Unless the law says otherwise, it is appealable as of right to the Court of Appeals. A declaratory judgment is final for appeal only if it meets this test.","c":"Wis. Stat. §§ 808.03(1), 806.04(1)"},{"t":"Appeal by permission: nonfinal orders","d":"A nonfinal judgment or order may be appealed only with leave of the Court of Appeals, granted if an immediate appeal will materially advance the termination of the litigation or clarify further proceedings, protect the petitioner from substantial or irreparable injury, or clarify an issue of general importance in the administration of justice.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 808.03(2); § 809.50"},{"t":"Class certification orders: appeal of right within 14 days","d":"An appellate court must hear an appeal of an order granting or denying class certification, or denying decertification, if the notice of appeal is filed within 14 days after entry. Discovery and other proceedings are stayed during that appeal, except the trial court may approve a settlement.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 803.08(11)(b)"},{"t":"What an appeal from the final judgment brings up","d":"An appeal from a final judgment or order brings before the court all prior nonfinal rulings adverse to the appellant and favorable to the respondent that were not previously appealed and decided.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 809.10(4)"}]],["Starting the appeal",[{"t":"Notice of appeal: 45 days with notice of entry, 90 days without","d":"File the notice of appeal with the clerk of the circuit court within 45 days of entry of the final judgment or order if written notice of entry was given within 21 days of entry; otherwise within 90 days. A notice filed before entry is treated as filed on the day of entry. If a party dies during the appeal period, the time is the normal period or 120 days after death, whichever is later. Shorter periods apply to some case types (e.g., 15 days for ch. 799 and s. 227.60 appeals, 20 days for s. 19.356 record-subject appeals).","c":"Wis. Stat. §§ 808.04(1)-(2), (6), (8), 806.06(3), (5), 809.10(1)(a)"},{"t":"Contents, docketing statement, and fee","d":"The notice must give the circuit court case name and number, identify the judgment or order and its entry date, say whether the case is one decided by a single judge under s. 752.31(2), and whether it has statutory preference. A docketing statement on the Court of Appeals form must accompany it (not required for self-represented parties and some case types). The $195 filing fee is paid to the clerk of the Court of Appeals; the circuit clerk cannot reject the notice for nonpayment. An inconsequential error in the notice is not jurisdictional.","c":"Wis. Stat. §§ 809.10(1)(b), (d), (f), 809.11(1), 809.25(2)(a)1."},{"t":"Ask for a 3-judge panel at the start in one-judge case types","d":"In case types decided by a single judge (small claims, ordinance and traffic, ch. 48/938, ch. 51/55, misdemeanors, forfeitures, contempt), an appellant who wants a 3-judge panel must file the motion with the notice of appeal; any other party within 14 days after service of the notice. Failing to file waives the request.","c":"Wis. Stat. §§ 752.31(2)-(3), 809.41(1)"},{"t":"Transcript: order it and file a statement on transcript in 14 days","d":"Within 14 days after filing the notice of appeal, the appellant must request and arrange payment for the transcript and file a statement on transcript in the circuit court (or state that none is needed). Other parties may designate more within 14 days after that. The reporter has 60 days to file and serve the transcript. The circuit clerk transmits the record within 20 days after the transcript is filed.","c":"Wis. Stat. §§ 809.11(4)-(7), 809.15(4)(a)"}]],["Post-judgment motions and the appeal clock",[{"t":"No tolling: motions after verdict are decided before judgment","d":"Motions after verdict (new trial, JNOV, change of answer) must be filed and served within 20 days after the verdict unless the court, within those 20 days, sets a longer schedule. If the court does not decide them within 90 days after the verdict, they are deemed denied and judgment is entered on the verdict. The 90-day decision period cannot be enlarged. The appeal then runs from entry of judgment.","c":"Wis. Stat. §§ 805.16(1)-(3), 805.14(5)(a), 801.15(2)(c)"},{"t":"Bench trials: motion to amend within 20 days after judgment","d":"In a trial to the court, a motion to amend findings, conclusions, or the judgment must be made within 20 days after entry of judgment. If not decided within 90 days after entry, it is deemed denied and the appeal time from the judgment starts 90 days after entry. This time cannot be enlarged.","c":"Wis. Stat. §§ 805.17(3), 801.15(2)(c)"},{"t":"Relief from judgment does not affect finality","d":"A s. 806.07 motion (mistake, new evidence, fraud, void judgment, etc.) must be made within a reasonable time, and within one year for mistake or fraud grounds. It does not affect finality or suspend the judgment, so it does not extend the appeal time. The circuit court may hear it while an appeal is pending.","c":"Wis. Stat. §§ 806.07(2), 808.07(2)(am), 808.075(1)"}]],["Cross-appeals and multiple appellants",[{"t":"Cross-appeal needed to change the judgment","d":"A respondent who seeks to modify the judgment or order appealed from, or another judgment or order in the same action, must file a notice of cross-appeal within the time for a notice of appeal or 30 days after the notice of appeal is filed, whichever is later. A cross-appellant has an appellant’s rights and duties.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 809.10(2)(b)"},{"t":"Joint and co-appeals; consolidation","d":"Parties entitled to appeal the same judgment may file a joint notice or proceed as a single appellant; otherwise they proceed as appellant and co-appellant with equal rights. The court may consolidate appeals from separate cases.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 809.10(2)(a), (3)"}]],["Briefs",[{"t":"Schedule: 40 / 30 / 15 days","d":"Appellant’s brief: 40 days after the record is filed in the Court of Appeals. Respondent’s brief: 30 days after the later of service of the appellant’s brief (plus 3 days if served by mail), its filing, or filing of the record. Reply (or a statement that none will be filed): 15 days after the later of service (plus 3 days for mail) or filing of the respondent’s brief.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 809.19(1), (3)(a), (4)(a)"},{"t":"Length: 11,000 words main briefs, 3,000 words reply","d":"Main briefs: 11,000 words in a proportional serif font, or 50 pages monospaced, counting the statement of the case, argument, and conclusion. Reply briefs and nonparty briefs: 3,000 words or 13 pages. Proportional font at least 13-point body text; certify form and length with the brief.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 809.19(8)(b)-(c), (8g)"},{"t":"Appendix and unpublished opinions","d":"The appellant files a short appendix with the circuit court’s findings or opinion, essential parts of the record showing the court’s reasoning, and a copy of any unpublished opinion cited, with a certification. Unpublished opinions issued on or after July 1, 2009 and authored by a judge may be cited only as persuasive authority.","c":"Wis. Stat. §§ 809.19(2)(a), (8g)(b), 809.23(3)"},{"t":"Amicus (nonparty) briefs need leave","d":"A nonparty must move for permission, stating its interest and why the brief is desirable. A brief supporting or opposing a petition for review or original action accompanies the motion and is due within the time for the opposing party’s response.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 809.19(7)"}]],["Stays, bonds, and motions",[{"t":"Appeal does not stay the judgment; move first in the trial court","d":"An appeal does not stay enforcement unless relief is granted. The trial or appellate court may stay enforcement, modify an injunction, or preserve the status quo, usually conditioned on an undertaking. Seek relief in the trial court first unless impractical. Injunction and receivership judgments are not stayed unless ordered.","c":"Wis. Stat. §§ 808.07(1)-(2), 809.12, 806.08(1), (3)"},{"t":"Supersedeas cap of $100 million","d":"In a civil action, the court sets the undertaking to stay execution for all appellants collectively, but it may not exceed $100,000,000, unless the appellee proves by a preponderance that the appellant is dissipating assets to avoid payment. Objections to a surety must be made within 14 days after service of the undertaking.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 808.07(2m), (6)"},{"t":"Motions practice","d":"Responses to motions are due 11 days after service. Motions that may affect the disposition or a brief, consolidation motions, transcript-related motions, and motions to supplement or correct the record automatically toll appellate deadlines while pending.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 809.14(1), (3)"}]],["Decision, costs, and reconsideration",[{"t":"Oral argument and publication","d":"The court decides whether to hear argument or decide on briefs. Publication is decided by a committee of Court of Appeals judges; one-judge decisions and most per curiam opinions are not published.","c":"Wis. Stat. §§ 809.22, 809.23(1)-(2)"},{"t":"Reconsideration in the Court of Appeals: 20 days, 1,100 words","d":"A motion for reconsideration may be filed within 20 days after the decision; it must state the points with particularity and may not exceed 1,100 words (5 pages monospaced). No response unless ordered. This time cannot be enlarged. A timely motion postpones the 30-day petition-for-review period until the court rules.","c":"Wis. Stat. §§ 809.24(1), 809.82(2)(e), 808.10(2)"},{"t":"Costs and frivolous-appeal sanctions","d":"Costs generally go against the losing side. File a statement of costs within 14 days of the decision; objections within 11 days. For an appeal taken for delay, the court may award double costs, a penalty up to 10% of the judgment, damages, and attorney fees; the motion must be filed by the time the respondent’s brief is filed. Failure to follow other rules is not jurisdictional but can lead to dismissal or summary reversal.","c":"Wis. Stat. §§ 809.25(1), (3), 809.83(1)-(2)"},{"t":"Remittitur","d":"The record and decision go back to the circuit court 31 days after the decision, stayed while a petition for review or reconsideration is pending. After remittitur, a party has one year to move for further proceedings when the mandate does not order specific action or a new trial. A party may file a new judge-substitution request within 20 days after the remittitur is filed when further trial-court proceedings are needed.","c":"Wis. Stat. §§ 809.26(1), 808.08(3), 801.58(7)"}]],["Supreme Court review",[{"t":"Petition for review: 30 days, 8,000 words","d":"File within 30 days of the Court of Appeals decision (or of the order on a timely reconsideration motion). Review is discretionary and granted only for special and important reasons (constitutional questions, policy, law development, conflicts, or precedent ripe for reexamination). The petition is limited to 8,000 words (35 pages monospaced) plus a separate appendix; no supporting memorandum is allowed. Response due 14 days after service.","c":"Wis. Stat. §§ 808.10, 809.62(1m), (1r), (2)-(4)"},{"t":"Petition for cross-review","d":"A party seeking to reverse or modify the Court of Appeals decision must file a petition for cross-review within the time for a petition for review or 30 days after another party’s petition is filed, whichever is later. Not needed merely to defend the result on other grounds.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 809.62(3m)"},{"t":"Bypass and certification","d":"A party may petition to bypass the Court of Appeals no later than 14 days after the respondent’s brief is filed; responses are due 14 days after service, and the petition stops the Court of Appeals from taking the case under submission. The Court of Appeals may also certify a case, or the Supreme Court may take it on its own motion.","c":"Wis. Stat. §§ 808.05, 809.60, 809.61"},{"t":"After review is granted; reconsideration","d":"Court of Appeals procedure applies in the Supreme Court unless the court orders otherwise. A motion for reconsideration of a Supreme Court decision is due within 20 days.","c":"Wis. Stat. §§ 809.63, 809.64"},{"t":"Original actions and supervisory writs","d":"The Supreme Court may be asked to take an original action or issue a supervisory writ by petition (ss. 809.70–809.71). The Court of Appeals has its own supervisory and prerogative writ jurisdiction; those petitions are limited to 8,000 words and responses are due 14 days after the court orders one.","c":"Wis. Stat. §§ 752.01(2), 752.02, 809.51, 809.70, 809.71"}]],["Interlocutory appeals",[{"t":"Petition for leave to appeal: 14 days","d":"File a petition (and any memorandum) in the Court of Appeals within 14 days after entry of the nonfinal order, limited to 8,000 words, with a copy of the order and a showing under the s. 808.03(2) criteria. Responses are due 14 days after service. If leave is granted, the order counts as the notice of appeal and a docketing statement is due within 11 days.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 809.50(1)-(3)"}]],["E-filing, time computation, and format",[{"t":"Appellate e-filing is mandatory for lawyers","d":"Wisconsin lawyers and PHV lawyers must register and e-file in the Supreme Court and Court of Appeals. A document is filed on a day if submission is completed by 11:59 p.m. Central time and the clerk later accepts it. Self-represented parties may opt in or file on paper.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 809.801(2)-(4)"},{"t":"Computing time; extensions","d":"Use s. 801.15(1) and (5): exclude weekends and holidays for periods under 11 days; add 3 days for mail service. The court may enlarge most deadlines for good cause, but not the time for a notice of appeal or cross-appeal from a final judgment (outside certain criminal and TPR appeals) or for reconsideration under s. 809.24.","c":"Wis. Stat. §§ 809.82(1)-(2), 801.15(1), (5)"},{"t":"Format","d":"Fit 8.5 x 11 paper, monospaced or proportional serif font, double-spaced, 1.25-inch side margins and 1-inch top and bottom, paginated at the bottom center. Captions use full party names with circuit and appellate designations; confidential matters use initials.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 809.81"}]],["Deadlines",[{"t":"Notice of appeal (final judgment or order), notice of entry given","d":"45 days from entry of the final judgment or order, if written notice of entry was given within 21 days of entry.\nJurisdictional; cannot be enlarged (s. 809.82(2)(b)).","c":"Wis. Stat. §§ 808.04(1), 806.06(5)","due":{"n":45,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the final judgment or order, if written notice of entry was given within 21 days of entry)","note":"Jurisdictional; cannot be enlarged (s. 809.82(2)(b))."}},{"t":"Notice of appeal (final judgment or order), no notice of entry","d":"90 days from entry of the final judgment or order.\nJurisdictional; cannot be enlarged.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 808.04(1)","due":{"n":90,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the final judgment or order)","note":"Jurisdictional; cannot be enlarged."}},{"t":"Notice of entry of judgment or order (to start the 45-day period)","d":"21 days from entry of the judgment or order.\nLate notice leaves the 90-day period in place.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 806.06(5)","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the judgment or order)","note":"Late notice leaves the 90-day period in place."}},{"t":"Notice of cross-appeal","d":"30 days from filing of the notice of appeal (or the full appeal period, if later).\nWhichever is later of the appeal period or 30 days after the notice of appeal.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 809.10(2)(b)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the notice of appeal)","note":"Whichever is later of the appeal period or 30 days after the notice of appeal."}},{"t":"Appeal of class certification order","d":"14 days from entry of the order.\nProceedings stayed during the appeal.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 803.08(11)(b)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order)","note":"Proceedings stayed during the appeal."}},{"t":"Petition for leave to appeal nonfinal order","d":"14 days from entry of the judgment or order.\n8,000-word limit; response due 14 days after service.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 809.50(1)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the judgment or order)","note":"8,000-word limit; response due 14 days after service."}},{"t":"Motions after verdict","d":"20 days from verdict.\nUnless the court sets a longer time by order within the 20 days.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 805.16(1)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (verdict)","note":"Unless the court sets a longer time by order within the 20 days."}},{"t":"Decision on motions after verdict (else deemed denied)","d":"90 days from verdict.\nCannot be enlarged (s. 801.15(2)(c)); judgment then entered on the verdict.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 805.16(3)","due":{"n":90,"from":"the trigger date (verdict)","note":"Cannot be enlarged (s. 801.15(2)(c)); judgment then entered on the verdict."}},{"t":"Motion to amend findings or judgment (bench trial)","d":"20 days from entry of judgment.\nDeemed denied if undecided 90 days after entry; appeal time then runs from day 90.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 805.17(3)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Deemed denied if undecided 90 days after entry; appeal time then runs from day 90."}},{"t":"Motion for new trial on newly discovered evidence","d":"365 days from verdict.\nDeemed denied unless decided within 90 days after the motion.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 805.16(4)","due":{"n":365,"from":"the trigger date (verdict)","note":"Deemed denied unless decided within 90 days after the motion."}},{"t":"Relief from judgment for mistake, excusable neglect, or fraud","d":"365 days from entry of judgment or making of the order or stipulation.\n‘Not more than one year’; other grounds within a reasonable time.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 806.07(2)","due":{"n":365,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment or making of the order or stipulation)","note":"‘Not more than one year’; other grounds within a reasonable time."}},{"t":"Statement on transcript; request and pay for transcript","d":"14 days from filing of the notice of appeal.\nFiled in the circuit court.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 809.11(4)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the notice of appeal)","note":"Filed in the circuit court."}},{"t":"Motion for 3-judge panel by a party other than appellant","d":"14 days from service of the notice of appeal.\nAppellant must file with the notice of appeal; failure waives.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 809.41(1)(d)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (service of the notice of appeal)","note":"Appellant must file with the notice of appeal; failure waives."}},{"t":"Appellant’s brief","d":"40 days from filing of the record in the Court of Appeals.\n11,000 words.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 809.19(1)","due":{"n":40,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the record in the Court of Appeals)","note":"11,000 words."}},{"t":"Respondent’s brief","d":"30 days from later of service (+3 days if mailed) or filing of appellant’s brief, or filing of the record.\n11,000 words.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 809.19(3)(a)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (later of service)","note":"11,000 words."}},{"t":"Reply brief or statement that none will be filed","d":"15 days from later of service (+3 days if mailed) or filing of respondent’s brief.\n3,000 words.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 809.19(4)(a)","due":{"n":15,"from":"the trigger date (later of service)","note":"3,000 words."}},{"t":"Response to a motion in the appellate court","d":"11 days from service of the motion.\n5 days in TPR appeals.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 809.14(1)","due":{"n":11,"from":"the trigger date (service of the motion)","note":"5 days in TPR appeals."}},{"t":"Petition to bypass the Court of Appeals","d":"14 days from filing of the respondent’s brief or response.\nResponse due 14 days after service.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 809.60(1)(a)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the respondent’s brief or response)","note":"Response due 14 days after service."}},{"t":"Motion for reconsideration in the Court of Appeals","d":"20 days from date of the decision.\nCannot be enlarged (s. 809.82(2)(e)); 1,100 words.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 809.24(1)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (date of the decision)","note":"Cannot be enlarged (s. 809.82(2)(e)); 1,100 words."}},{"t":"Statement of costs in the Court of Appeals","d":"14 days from filing of the decision.\nObjection by motion within 11 days after service.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 809.25(1)(c)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the decision)","note":"Objection by motion within 11 days after service."}},{"t":"Petition for review in the Supreme Court","d":"30 days from date of the Court of Appeals decision (or order on timely reconsideration).\n8,000 words; review discretionary.","c":"Wis. Stat. §§ 808.10, 809.62(1m)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (date of the Court of Appeals decision)","note":"8,000 words; review discretionary."}},{"t":"Response to petition for review","d":"14 days from service of the petition.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 809.62(3)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (service of the petition)","note":""}},{"t":"Petition for cross-review","d":"30 days from filing of another party’s petition for review (or the review period, if later).\nWhichever is later.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 809.62(3m)(a)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (filing of another party’s petition for review)","note":"Whichever is later."}},{"t":"Motion for reconsideration in the Supreme Court","d":"20 days from date of the Supreme Court decision.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 809.64","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (date of the Supreme Court decision)","note":""}},{"t":"Substitution of judge after remand","d":"20 days from filing of the remittitur in the trial court.\nOnly when further trial-court proceedings are needed after reversal, modification, or new trial.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 801.58(7)","due":{"n":20,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the remittitur in the trial court)","note":"Only when further trial-court proceedings are needed after reversal, modification, or new trial."}},{"t":"Motion for further proceedings after remittitur","d":"365 days from receipt of the remitted record by the trial court clerk.\nApplies when the mandate orders neither specific action nor a new trial; otherwise dismissal.","c":"Wis. Stat. § 808.08(3)","due":{"n":365,"from":"the trigger date (receipt of the remitted record by the trial court clerk)","note":"Applies when the mandate orders neither specific action nor a new trial; otherwise dismissal."}}]]],"dl":[["Notice of appeal (final judgment or order), notice of entry given",45,"Entry of the final judgment or order, if written notice of entry was given within 21 days of entry","Wis. Stat. §§ 808.04(1), 806.06(5)","Jurisdictional; cannot be enlarged (s. 809.82(2)(b))."],["Notice of appeal (final judgment or order), no notice of entry",90,"Entry of the final judgment or order","Wis. Stat. § 808.04(1)","Jurisdictional; cannot be enlarged."],["Notice of entry of judgment or order (to start the 45-day period)",21,"Entry of the judgment or order","Wis. Stat. § 806.06(5)","Late notice leaves the 90-day period in place."],["Notice of cross-appeal",30,"Filing of the notice of appeal (or the full appeal period, if later)","Wis. Stat. § 809.10(2)(b)","Whichever is later of the appeal period or 30 days after the notice of appeal."],["Appeal of class certification order",14,"Entry of the order","Wis. Stat. § 803.08(11)(b)","Proceedings stayed during the appeal."],["Petition for leave to appeal nonfinal order",14,"Entry of the judgment or order","Wis. Stat. § 809.50(1)","8,000-word limit; response due 14 days after service."],["Motions after verdict",20,"Verdict","Wis. Stat. § 805.16(1)","Unless the court sets a longer time by order within the 20 days."],["Decision on motions after verdict (else deemed denied)",90,"Verdict","Wis. Stat. § 805.16(3)","Cannot be enlarged (s. 801.15(2)(c)); judgment then entered on the verdict."],["Motion to amend findings or judgment (bench trial)",20,"Entry of judgment","Wis. Stat. § 805.17(3)","Deemed denied if undecided 90 days after entry; appeal time then runs from day 90."],["Motion for new trial on newly discovered evidence",365,"Verdict","Wis. Stat. § 805.16(4)","Deemed denied unless decided within 90 days after the motion."],["Relief from judgment for mistake, excusable neglect, or fraud",365,"Entry of judgment or making of the order or stipulation","Wis. Stat. § 806.07(2)","‘Not more than one year’; other grounds within a reasonable time."],["Statement on transcript; request and pay for transcript",14,"Filing of the notice of appeal","Wis. Stat. § 809.11(4)","Filed in the circuit court."],["Motion for 3-judge panel by a party other than appellant",14,"Service of the notice of appeal","Wis. Stat. § 809.41(1)(d)","Appellant must file with the notice of appeal; failure waives."],["Appellant’s brief",40,"Filing of the record in the Court of Appeals","Wis. Stat. § 809.19(1)","11,000 words."],["Respondent’s brief",30,"Later of service (+3 days if mailed) or filing of appellant’s brief, or filing of the record","Wis. Stat. § 809.19(3)(a)","11,000 words."],["Reply brief or statement that none will be filed",15,"Later of service (+3 days if mailed) or filing of respondent’s brief","Wis. Stat. § 809.19(4)(a)","3,000 words."],["Response to a motion in the appellate court",11,"Service of the motion","Wis. Stat. § 809.14(1)","5 days in TPR appeals."],["Petition to bypass the Court of Appeals",14,"Filing of the respondent’s brief or response","Wis. Stat. § 809.60(1)(a)","Response due 14 days after service."],["Motion for reconsideration in the Court of Appeals",20,"Date of the decision","Wis. Stat. § 809.24(1)","Cannot be enlarged (s. 809.82(2)(e)); 1,100 words."],["Statement of costs in the Court of Appeals",14,"Filing of the decision","Wis. Stat. § 809.25(1)(c)","Objection by motion within 11 days after service."],["Petition for review in the Supreme Court",30,"Date of the Court of Appeals decision (or order on timely reconsideration)","Wis. Stat. §§ 808.10, 809.62(1m)","8,000 words; review discretionary."],["Response to petition for review",14,"Service of the petition","Wis. Stat. § 809.62(3)",""],["Petition for cross-review",30,"Filing of another party’s petition for review (or the review period, if later)","Wis. Stat. § 809.62(3m)(a)","Whichever is later."],["Motion for reconsideration in the Supreme Court",20,"Date of the Supreme Court decision","Wis. Stat. § 809.64",""],["Substitution of judge after remand",20,"Filing of the remittitur in the trial court","Wis. Stat. § 801.58(7)","Only when further trial-court proceedings are needed after reversal, modification, or new trial."],["Motion for further proceedings after remittitur",365,"Receipt of the remitted record by the trial court clerk","Wis. Stat. § 808.08(3)","Applies when the mandate orders neither specific action nor a new trial; otherwise dismissal."]],"courts":[],"links":[["Official site","https://www.wicourts.gov/"]]},"rulesName":"Wisconsin Statutes chs. 801–806","rulesSrc":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801","rulesAsof":"as checked November 10, 2026","rulesNote":"Each entry is our summary of the rule, not the rule itself. Tap Read the official text before you rely on it.","rules":[{"n":"801.01","t":"Kinds of proceedings; scope of chs. 801 to 847","s":"Proceedings are actions or special proceedings. Chapters 801–847 govern all civil actions and special proceedings in circuit court unless a statute or rule prescribes a different procedure, and are construed to secure the just, speedy and inexpensive determination of every action (like FRCP 1).","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"801.02","t":"Commencement of action","s":"An action is commenced as to a defendant when the summons and complaint are filed, provided an authenticated copy is served on that defendant within 90 days after filing (unlike FRCP 3–4(m), the 90 days cannot be extended: s. 801.15(2)(a)). No service before filing. Fees are paid at filing. Prisoner suits about prison conditions require exhaustion of administrative remedies, and a prisoner with 3 prior dismissals for frivolousness etc. cannot proceed without prepaying fees unless in imminent danger of serious physical injury.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"801.03–801.04","t":"Jurisdiction definitions; jurisdictional requirements","s":"Defines defendant, person and plaintiff. Subject-matter jurisdiction comes from the constitution and statutes and cannot be conferred by consent. A personal judgment requires a jurisdictional ground under s. 801.05 or 801.06 plus service under s. 801.11 (or waiver); in rem or quasi in rem judgments proceed under ss. 801.07 and 801.12.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"801.05","t":"Personal jurisdiction, grounds for generally (long-arm statute)","s":"Lists the grounds for personal jurisdiction over a served defendant: presence, domicile, domestic entity, or substantial and not isolated activities in Wisconsin; local acts or omissions; local injury from a foreign act with solicitation or local use of products; local services, goods or contracts; local property; deficiency judgments; officers of Wisconsin corporations; taxes; insurance; certain family and restraining-order actions; and claims against a personal representative. Claims based on specific grounds cannot be joined with unrelated claims lacking their own ground.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"801.06–801.08","t":"Jurisdiction without summons; in rem grounds; objection to jurisdiction","s":"A plaintiff is subject to jurisdiction on counterclaims, and a defendant who appears without raising the defense waives it (s. 802.06(8)). In rem or quasi in rem jurisdiction exists over property in the state, mortgage foreclosures, attached property, public nuisances, and marital status. Personal jurisdiction objections are tried by the court without a jury before the merits, and those findings do not bind the merits trial.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"801.09","t":"Summons, contents of","s":"The summons must give the court, county, case classification code, and party names and addresses, and direct the defendant to answer (or demand the complaint) within 20 days of personal or substituted service, 45 days if the state is the defendant or if an insurer is a defendant or any claim sounds in tort, or 40 days after first publication. It must be signed by hand by the plaintiff or attorney, with the attorney’s bar number. Authentication is the clerk’s filing stamp with the case number.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"801.095","t":"Summons form","s":"Prescribes four statutory summons forms: personal service with or without the complaint attached, and service without personal service (publication) with or without the complaint. Each warns that judgment may be entered and may become a lien on real estate or be enforced by garnishment.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"801.10","t":"Summons, by whom served; proof","s":"Any adult nonparty resident of the state where service is made may serve, and adult nonparty residents of Illinois, Iowa, Michigan or Minnesota may also serve in Wisconsin. The server endorses the summons and makes proof of service; if service is challenged, proof is by the server’s affidavit (showing reasonable diligence for substituted service), the sheriff’s certificate, a publisher’s affidavit plus mailing affidavit, or the defendant’s written admission.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"801.11","t":"Personal jurisdiction, manner of serving summons","s":"Individuals: personal service in or out of state; only if that fails with reasonable diligence, leaving a copy at the usual abode with a competent family member at least 14 or a competent adult resident; only if both fail, publication as a class 3 notice plus mailing. Separate rules for minors under 14 and persons under guardianship, the state (Attorney General), counties, towns, cities, villages and school districts. Corporations and LLCs: an officer, director or managing agent, or the person apparently in charge of that person’s office; publication if diligence fails; or a registered agent. Insurers: service on an agent is valid only if a copy is mailed by registered mail to the insurer within 5 days.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"801.12–801.13","t":"In rem service; notice of object of action; when service is deemed made","s":"In rem defendants may be served under s. 801.11 or, if unknown, by publication, with the complaint or a notice of object of action describing the property. Personal or substituted service is effective the day it is made; publication is effective on the first day of required publication.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"801.14","t":"Service and filing of pleadings and other papers","s":"Every pleading, discovery paper, written motion, notice and similar paper must be served on each party (parties in default need new-claim pleadings served as a summons). Serve the attorney of a represented party, by hand, mail, fax, or leaving at the office or abode; email service is allowed only with written consent (e-filing service is governed by s. 801.18). Mail service is complete on mailing; fax and email on transmission. When a limited-scope lawyer appears, serve both client and lawyer. Papers are filed within a reasonable time after service, and filing certifies service.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"801.145","t":"Form of papers","s":"Papers other than exhibits and wills may be no larger than 8.5 by 11 inches; the clerk returns nonconforming papers.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"801.15","t":"Time","s":"Exclude the trigger day; include the last day unless the clerk’s office is closed. For periods under 11 days, exclude Saturdays, Sundays and holidays (including all of Good Friday). The court may enlarge time on motion for cause; after expiration only for excusable neglect. Not enlargeable: the 90-day service period, the time to appeal, the time to decide post-verdict motions, and the times for s. 805.17(3) and s. 806.07 motions. Written motions and notice of hearing must be served 5 days before the hearing; opposing affidavits 1 day before. Add 3 days for mail service; add 1 day for fax, email or e-filing service completed between 5 p.m. and midnight.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"801.16","t":"Filing; facsimile filing","s":"Papers are filed with the clerk of circuit court, and the judge may require a copy. Fax filing of no-fee papers is allowed under a chief-judge-approved local rule (15-page limit unless the judge approves) or with the assigned judge’s permission.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"801.18","t":"Electronic filing (circuit court)","s":"Licensed Wisconsin lawyers, lawyers admitted pro hac vice (after the PHV order), and high-volume filers must register and e-file all new filings; self-represented parties may opt in or use paper. Users must opt in on each case or receive no notices. A document is filed the day it is submitted by 11:59 p.m. Central time if later accepted by the clerk, but this does not change time computations. The notice of activity is service on other users; paper parties are served by traditional methods, and documents requiring personal service still need it. Electronic signatures read ‘Electronically signed by’; the court may grant relief for system failures.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"801.19–801.21","t":"Protected information; confidential filings; motions to seal","s":"Parties must omit or redact Social Security, taxpayer ID, financial account, driver license and passport numbers from filings and submit them on the protected information form when required; the court does not check. Documents on the Director of State Courts’ confidential list are automatically confidential if properly identified at filing. Otherwise, sealing or redaction requires a served motion stating the authority; the court uses the least restrictive means. Transcript redaction motions are due 30 days after the transcript is filed.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"801.50","t":"Venue","s":"Venue lies where the claim arose, where the property at issue is, or where a defendant resides or does substantial business; otherwise in any county the plaintiff designates. Suits against the state are venued where the plaintiff designates (Dane County for prisoners); special rules for rule challenges, habeas corpus, redistricting and other matters. A venue defect does not invalidate any order or judgment.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"801.51–801.53","t":"Challenges to venue; discretionary change","s":"Improper venue is raised by a motion for change of venue made at or before the first motion or responsive pleading, or later if grounds could not be found with diligence. The court may change venue at any time in the interest of justice or for convenience (election and ethics forfeiture cases follow criminal venue rules). Venue motions are decided on submissions, with any argument by telephone unless ordered otherwise.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"801.54","t":"Discretionary transfer to tribal court","s":"Where a circuit court and a Wisconsin tribal court have concurrent jurisdiction, the circuit court may, after notice and hearing, transfer the action to the tribal court after weighing listed factors (including whether tribal law must be interpreted), unless all parties stipulate.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"801.56","t":"Change of venue if judge disqualified by interest","s":"If the judge is a party, interested, related to a party, or was counsel for a party, the court must change the place of trial or call in another judge on any party’s application, and may do so on its own.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"801.58","t":"Substitution of judge","s":"Each party (parties united in interest count as one) gets one peremptory request to substitute the assigned judge, filed before any preliminary contested matter is heard and within 60 days after filing (plaintiff) or service (others). A newly assigned trial judge: 10 days after notice, or 24 hours if notice comes within 10 days of trial. If timely and proper, the judge loses jurisdiction; a ruling of untimeliness is reviewable by the chief judge if requested within 10 days. After reversal or remand for further proceedings, a new request may be filed within 20 days after the remittitur. No federal counterpart.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"801.59","t":"Assigned judge","s":"When a judge is assigned under s. 751.03, the clerk notifies the parties, and a substitution request against that judge must be filed within 7 days after notice of the assignment.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"801.61–801.62","t":"Proceedings after change of venue; conclusiveness","s":"The clerk transmits the file to the new county; if transmission and filing are not made within 20 days after the order, the moving party loses the change. After trial in the new court, the change is conclusive unless a remand motion was made before trial; a reversed change may be renewed within 20 days after the papers return.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"801.63","t":"Stay to permit trial in a foreign forum","s":"On motion, the court may stay an action that in substantial justice should be tried outside Wisconsin (forum non conveniens), on conditions such as consent to jurisdiction and waiver of limitations. The court keeps jurisdiction for 5 years after the last order affecting the stay; then the clerk enters dismissal.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"801.64","t":"Legislative findings; 2007 Wisconsin Act 1","s":"Findings supporting a place of trial in the county where an offender resides under 2007 Wisconsin Act 1 (ethics and election enforcement); not a general procedure rule.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"802.01","t":"Pleadings allowed; form of motions","s":"Pleadings are the complaint, answer, reply to a counterclaim, answer to a cross claim, and third-party complaint and answer; no others unless ordered. Motions must be in writing with particular grounds and relief sought, and supporting papers must be served with the notice of motion. Orders to show cause may not be used unless a statute authorizes them.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"802.02","t":"General rules of pleading","s":"A claim requires a short and plain statement identifying the transaction or occurrence and showing entitlement to relief, plus a demand for judgment. Unlike FRCP 8, a tort claim for money may not state the amount sought (s. 802.02(1m)). Lists required affirmative defenses (including contributory negligence, superseding cause, and the statute of limitations). Averments not denied are admitted, except those about the fact, nature and extent of injury and damage.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"802.025","t":"Asbestos trust claims in personal injury actions","s":"In asbestos-related personal injury actions, the plaintiff must give all parties a sworn statement of asbestos-trust claims within 45 days after joinder of issues and trust claim materials within 60 days, and supplement within 30 days of new claims; the court enters a trust claims document at least 30 days before trial. Collection of damages is conditioned on assignment of trust claims.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"802.03","t":"Pleading special matters","s":"Capacity is denied by specific negative averment or motion. Fraud and mistake must be pleaded with particularity. Conditions precedent may be pleaded generally but denied with particularity. In libel or slander, the complaint must set out the particular words. In goods or services claims, the plaintiff may itemize; otherwise must give an itemized statement within 10 days of a written demand.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"802.04","t":"Form of pleadings","s":"Every pleading needs a caption with court, venue, title, file number and designation; the complaint caption includes the case classification code. Claims are stated in numbered paragraphs, and exhibits are part of the pleading.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"802.045","t":"Limited scope representation","s":"A lawyer may appear for limited proceedings or issues by filing and serving a notice of limited appearance before or at the proceeding; it is not a general appearance. Both client and lawyer are served. The role ends without court order when the lawyer files and serves a notice of termination certifying the work and court-ordered acts are done. No FRCP counterpart.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"802.05","t":"Signing; representations to court; sanctions","s":"Mirrors FRCP 11: every paper is signed with the signer’s address, phone, email and bar number, and a lawyer must give the court a primary email address. A lawyer drafting for a self-represented person need not sign, but the paper must say ‘This document was prepared with the assistance of a lawyer.’ Sanctions motions have a 21-day safe harbor. Monetary sanctions are not available against a represented party for legal contentions. Courts must screen and may dismiss frivolous prisoner suits (s. 802.05(4)).","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"802.06","t":"Defenses and objections; motions","s":"Answer within 20 days after service of the complaint; 45 days if an insurer is a defendant, any claim sounds in tort, or the state is a defendant (same for replies and cross-claim answers). Ten listed defenses may be raised by pre-answer motion, including res judicata, statute of limitations, and another action pending, beyond the FRCP 12(b) list; venue is raised under s. 801.51. A motion to dismiss for failure to state a claim, for judgment on the pleadings, or for a more definite statement stays discovery for 180 days or until decided unless particularized discovery is shown necessary (no FRCP counterpart). After a denied motion, answer within 10 days. Personal-jurisdiction and service defenses are waived if omitted.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"802.07","t":"Counterclaim and cross claim","s":"A defendant may counterclaim any claim against the plaintiff; unlike FRCP 13(a), the statute does not label any counterclaim compulsory. Cross claims must arise from the same transaction or concern the same property. Additional parties may be joined, and separate judgments may be entered.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"802.08","t":"Summary judgment","s":"A party may move within 8 months after the summons and complaint are filed, or within the time in a scheduling order. Serve the motion at least 20 days before the hearing; opposing affidavits at least 5 days before, unless the scheduling order sets earlier times. The standard matches FRCP 56 (no genuine issue of material fact). Affidavits must be on personal knowledge with admissible facts; the opponent cannot rest on the pleadings. Bad-faith affidavits draw expenses and fees. Summary judgment may be granted to the nonmoving party.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"802.09","t":"Amended and supplemental pleadings","s":"A party may amend once as of course within 6 months after the summons and complaint are filed (or as the scheduling order sets); after that, only with leave (freely given) or written consent. Respond within 20 days (45 days in tort, insurer or state cases). Amendments to conform to the evidence and relation back follow FRCP 15, including relation back for a changed party who had notice within the limitations period.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"802.10","t":"Calendar practice; scheduling and pretrial","s":"Except in exempt case types (e.g., small claims, family, certiorari, probate), the court may enter a scheduling order after consulting counsel, covering joinder, amendment, motions, discovery, experts, ESI, ADR, and, within 30 days after entry of the order, the mode of trial including the jury demand and fee. Pretrial conferences address issues, evidence and settlement; attorneys must have authority to stipulate. Violations are sanctionable under ss. 802.05, 804.12, 805.03 and 895.044.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"802.12","t":"Alternative dispute resolution","s":"Defines settlement alternatives (binding and nonbinding arbitration, mediation, early neutral evaluation, mini-trial, summary jury trial, etc.). The judge may order parties to select one and may require personal participation, but without consent may not order arbitration or summary jury trial, and the order may not delay the schedule. Aggrieved parties get a hearing.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"803.01","t":"Parties; real party in interest; capacity; guardians ad litem","s":"No dismissal for lack of the real party in interest until a reasonable time is allowed for ratification, joinder or substitution. Minors and incompetent persons appear by guardian ad litem; a defendant minor 14 or older may apply within 20 days after service, otherwise the court appoints.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"803.02","t":"Joinder of claims and remedies","s":"A party may join as many claims as it has against an opposing party, and claims normally cognizable only after another (e.g., a creditor’s claim to set aside a fraudulent conveyance) may be joined.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"803.03","t":"Joinder of persons needed for just adjudication","s":"Follows FRCP 19: a person must be joined if feasible when complete relief requires it or the person’s interest may be impaired or others exposed to inconsistent obligations; if joinder is not feasible, the court decides whether to proceed. Unlike FRCP 19, a claimant must also join everyone holding subrogated, derivative (including loss of consortium) or partially assigned claims; special rules apply to public assistance recipients and the Department of Health Services when medical assistance is involved.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"803.04","t":"Permissive joinder of parties","s":"Plaintiffs and defendants may be joined when claims arise from the same transaction or series and share a common question (like FRCP 20). In negligence actions, an insurer with an interest adverse to the plaintiff or that controls the defense may be joined directly as a defendant (s. 803.04(2)), a significant difference from federal practice. The court may order separate trials.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"803.045","t":"Actions to satisfy spousal obligations","s":"A creditor suing on a family-purpose or marital-property obligation under s. 766.55(2) may proceed against the obligated or incurring spouse or both, and against the other spouse in limited circumstances.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"803.05","t":"Third-party practice","s":"A defending party may implead a person who is or may be liable to it for the plaintiff’s claim, or a necessary party under s. 803.03, without leave if served within 6 months after the summons and complaint are filed (or by the scheduling order deadline); after that, by motion. The third-party defendant may assert defenses and claims as in FRCP 14.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"803.06–803.07","t":"Misjoinder and nonjoinder; interpleader","s":"Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal; parties may be added or dropped and claims severed. Claimants against the plaintiff may be joined and required to interplead when the plaintiff faces double or multiple liability; a defendant may obtain interpleader by counterclaim or cross claim.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"803.08","t":"Class actions","s":"Rewritten to track FRCP 23: numerosity, commonality, typicality, adequacy, and the three types of class actions; certification order, notice, settlement approval, objectors, and class counsel. At least 50 percent of residual funds go to the Wisconsin Trust Account Foundation. An order granting or denying certification or denying decertification is appealable as of right if the notice of appeal is filed within 14 days after entry, and all proceedings are stayed during that appeal (broader than FRCP 23(f)’s discretionary review).","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"803.09","t":"Intervention","s":"Intervention of right and permissive intervention follow FRCP 24. The Assembly, Senate and Legislature may intervene as of right at any time when a party challenges the constitutionality, validity or construction of a statute (s. 803.09(2m), with s. 13.365). A motion to intervene must be served with a pleading stating the claim or defense.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"803.10","t":"Substitution of parties","s":"On death, a motion to substitute must be made within 90 days after death is suggested on the record or the action is dismissed as to the decedent. Also covers incompetency, transfer of interest, and public officers who leave office.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"804.01","t":"General provisions governing discovery","s":"Scope: nonprivileged matter relevant to a claim or defense and proportional to the needs of the case (as in FRCP 26(b)(1)). There are no FRCP 26(a)-style initial disclosures, but litigation-funding agreements must be disclosed without a request (s. 804.01(2)(bg)). Testifying experts are discovered by interrogatory and deposition; consulting experts only on exceptional circumstances. Certain inaccessible ESI need not be produced absent substantial need and good cause, and ESI requests may not be served until the parties confer. Covers protective orders, supplementation, custody of discovery materials (not filed), and clawback of inadvertently produced privileged material.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"804.015","t":"Limits on discovery by prisoners","s":"A prisoner may not obtain discovery until the court receives the answer, and none while a motion to dismiss or for summary judgment is pending, unless the court orders otherwise.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"804.02","t":"Perpetuation of testimony","s":"Before an action, a verified petition may be filed to depose a witness to perpetuate testimony, with notice served at least 20 days before the hearing. Pending appeal, the trial court may allow depositions to perpetuate testimony.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"804.03–804.04","t":"Persons before whom depositions are taken; stipulations","s":"Depositions are taken before an officer authorized to administer oaths where taken, or a court-appointed person; rules for foreign depositions and disqualification for interest. Parties may stipulate in writing to modify deposition and discovery procedures unless the court orders otherwise.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"804.045","t":"Limits on number of depositions","s":"Absent stipulation or order, each party is limited to 10 depositions, none longer than 7 hours (FRCP 30 sets 10 depositions per side and one 7-hour day).","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"804.05","t":"Depositions upon oral examination","s":"Any party may depose any person after commencement (prisoners only with leave). Reasonable written notice to all parties; party deponents may be compelled by notice; nonparties by subpoena. Provides for organizational (designee) depositions, remote and non-stenographic recording, objections, and termination motions. The deponent has 30 days to read and sign the transcript.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"804.06","t":"Depositions upon written questions","s":"After notice and service of questions, cross questions are due within 30 days, redirect within 10 days after cross, and recross within 10 days after redirect.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"804.07","t":"Use of depositions in court proceedings","s":"Depositions are usable against parties present or noticed: to impeach, an adverse party’s or its designee’s for any purpose, and a nonparty’s if unavailable (dead, more than 30 miles away or out of state, ill, or not subpoenable). Unlike the federal rule, any party may use a medical expert’s deposition for any purpose. Objections to the form of written questions must be served within the time for the next questions and within 5 days after the last questions.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"804.08","t":"Interrogatories to parties","s":"Limited to 25 including subparts absent stipulation or order. Answer and object within 30 days after service, except a defendant has 45 days after service of the summons and complaint. Business records may be produced instead when the burden is equal.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"804.09","t":"Production of documents and inspection","s":"Requests to produce documents, ESI and things or to enter land; absent stipulation or order, requests are limited to a reasonable time period not exceeding 5 years before the claim accrued (no federal counterpart). Respond in writing within 30 days, or 45 days after service of the summons and complaint for a defendant. Covers form of ESI production. Nonparties are reached by subpoena (s. 805.07).","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"804.10","t":"Physical and mental examinations; medical records","s":"The court may order a physical, mental or vocational examination for cause when a party’s condition is in issue. In personal injury actions the claimant must give access to X-rays and medical records. Reports must be exchanged, and a report is inadmissible unless copies of all reports received are delivered within 10 days of receipt.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"804.11","t":"Requests for admission","s":"Matters are admitted unless answered or objected to within 30 days after service, but a defendant need not respond until 45 days after service of the summons and complaint. Denials must fairly meet the request; lack of knowledge requires reasonable inquiry. Admissions may be withdrawn or amended on motion; unreasonable failure to admit leads to expenses.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"804.12","t":"Failure to make discovery; sanctions","s":"Motions to compel, with mandatory expenses to the winner unless substantially justified. For violation of an order: deemed facts, preclusion, striking pleadings, dismissal, default, contempt, and expenses. A party’s failure to appear for its own noticed deposition, or to serve any answers or response to interrogatories or inspection requests, or to supplement, allows sanctions without a prior order. Includes ESI safe harbor for routine good-faith system operation.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"805.01","t":"Jury trial of right","s":"The constitutional and statutory jury right is preserved. A party must demand a jury (in writing or orally on the record) at or before the scheduling or pretrial conference, whichever comes first, or the right is waived; payment of the jury fee is set in the scheduling order (s. 802.10(3)(e)). A jury demand may not be withdrawn without the parties’ consent.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"805.02","t":"Advisory jury and trial by consent","s":"In nonjury actions the court may use an advisory jury, or with both parties’ consent a jury with the effect of a jury of right.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"805.03","t":"Failure to prosecute or comply with procedure","s":"The court may make just orders, including s. 804.12 sanctions and dismissal, for failure to prosecute or obey statutes or orders. A dismissal is on the merits unless the order states otherwise for good cause. A dismissal on the merits may be set aside under s. 806.07; one not on the merits for good cause within a reasonable time.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"805.04","t":"Voluntary dismissal","s":"The plaintiff may dismiss without order before service of a responsive pleading or motion, or by stipulation of all appearing parties; it is without prejudice unless the plaintiff has previously dismissed an action including the same claim (two-dismissal rule). Otherwise dismissal requires a court order on terms. Costs of a previously dismissed action may be ordered.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"805.05","t":"Consolidation; separate trials","s":"Actions that could have been joined may be consolidated or tried jointly, including transfer between counties by joint order of both courts. The court may order separate trials of claims or issues for convenience, to avoid prejudice, or for economy.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"805.06","t":"Referees","s":"The court may appoint a referee and fix fees. Hearings begin within 20 days after the order of reference unless it provides otherwise. Objections to the referee’s report are due within 10 days after notice of its filing.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"805.07","t":"Subpoena","s":"Subpoenas issue under ch. 885, and any attorney of record may issue one for deposition, hearing or trial. Documents and ESI may be commanded. Notice of a third-party discovery subpoena must go to all parties at least 10 days before the scheduled deposition, and the materials may not be produced before the subpoena date unless all parties agree. The court may quash or modify oppressive subpoenas and condition them on payment of costs.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"805.08","t":"Jurors","s":"The court examines jurors under oath for relationship, interest, opinion and bias; parties may supplement voir dire. Each side gets 3 peremptory challenges, exercised alternately starting with the plaintiff; adverse co-defendants may get up to 3 more, and each side gets one more when extra jurors are selected. Extra jurors are excused by lot before deliberation.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"805.09","t":"Jury size; five-sixths verdict","s":"The jury’s size is set by s. 756.06(2)(b) (not collected). A verdict agreed to by five-sixths of the jurors is the verdict, and the same five-sixths must agree on all questions needed for a verdict on a claim. No unanimity requirement in civil cases.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"805.10","t":"Examination of witnesses; arguments","s":"Unless the judge orders otherwise, one attorney per side examines a witness and no more than 2 per side argue. The plaintiff opens and closes; rebuttal is limited to matters raised by the opponent. The court may limit argument time.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"805.11","t":"Objections; exceptions","s":"A party with fair opportunity to object before a ruling must do so with specific grounds or the error is waived; formal exceptions are abolished. Evidentiary objections follow s. 901.03.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"805.12","t":"Special verdicts","s":"Unless it orders otherwise, the court directs a special verdict of written questions on ultimate facts (Wisconsin’s default, unlike FRCP 49’s option). In negligence cases each respect of negligence need not be submitted separately. An omitted issue not raised by any party is deemed found by the court consistent with the judgment.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"805.13","t":"Jury instructions; note-taking; form of verdict","s":"Statements by the judge to the jury must be on the record. The court decides whether jurors may take notes and may give preliminary instructions. At the instruction and verdict conference, counsel must object with particularity on the record, or errors in the proposed instructions or verdict are waived. The jury gets one complete set of written instructions.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"805.14","t":"Sufficiency of evidence; motions after verdict","s":"A challenge to sufficiency succeeds only if there is no credible evidence to sustain a finding for the nonmovant. Involuntary nonsuit is abolished. Motions to dismiss at the close of plaintiff’s case, for directed verdict at the close of all evidence, and after verdict for JNOV, to change answers, and for judgment on the verdict (not required).","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"805.15","t":"New trials","s":"Grounds: trial errors, verdict contrary to law or the weight of evidence, excessive or inadequate damages, newly discovered evidence, or the interest of justice; orders must state the reasons. Newly discovered evidence requires diligence, materiality and a probable different result. For excessive or inadequate damages, the court sets a reasonable amount and gives the party 10 days to accept remittitur or additur or face a new damages trial. Conditional rulings on alternate motions are required.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"805.16","t":"Time for motions after verdict","s":"Motions after verdict must be filed and served within 20 days after the verdict unless the court sets a longer schedule within those 20 days; hearing between 10 and 60 days after verdict. If not decided within 90 days after the verdict, the motion is deemed denied and judgment is entered (not extendable). A newly-discovered-evidence motion may be made within one year after verdict and is deemed denied if not decided within 90 days. Differs from FRCP 50 and 59, which run 28 days from entry of judgment.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"805.17","t":"Trial to the court","s":"After the plaintiff rests, the defendant may move to dismiss. The court finds ultimate facts and states conclusions of law, which are reviewed for clear error, within 60 days after submission. A motion to amend findings or the judgment, or for a new trial, is due within 20 days after entry of judgment; if not decided within 90 days after entry, it is deemed denied and the appeal time runs from day 90.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"805.18","t":"Harmless error","s":"Courts disregard errors that do not affect substantial rights, and no judgment is reversed or new trial granted for jury, evidence, pleading or procedure errors unless substantial rights were affected.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"806.01","t":"Judgment","s":"A judgment determines the action and may be final or interlocutory. It grants all relief to which the party is entitled even if not demanded, but on default may not exceed the complaint’s demand; in tort money claims the plaintiff must specify the amount before judgment. Partial and interlocutory judgments are allowed.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"806.02","t":"Default judgment","s":"When no issue has been joined and the time has expired, a party may move for default judgment on proof of service and an affidavit of default; appearing defendants get notice. If the defendant never appeared, the court requires proof of service and of facts showing personal jurisdiction. The clerk may enter judgment on an express contract for a liquidated sum after personal service (not abode service). Default is also available against a party who appeared but fails to appear at trial.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"806.025","t":"Payment of judgments to prisoners","s":"A money judgment for a prisoner is applied first to unpaid restitution and crime-victim judgments, then to other listed obligations.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"806.03","t":"Judgment on admitted claim","s":"On an express contract for a liquidated sum, the clerk enters judgment for any amount the answer admits, and the court may order payment of the admitted part.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"806.04","t":"Uniform Declaratory Judgments Act","s":"Courts may declare rights, status and legal relations under instruments, statutes, ordinances and contracts, before or after breach, with the effect of a final judgment, but appeal finality is governed by s. 808.03(1). All persons with an interest affected must be made parties, and the Attorney General must be served when a statute’s validity is challenged.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"806.05","t":"Declaratory judgments against obscene matter","s":"A special in rem proceeding in which a district attorney seeks a declaration that specific matter is obscene, with notice, hearing and jury rights.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"806.06","t":"Rendition, perfection and entry of judgment","s":"A judgment is rendered when signed, entered when filed with the clerk, perfected by taxing costs, and granted when given orally on the record. The prevailing party must perfect within 30 days of entry or lose costs. Any party may serve notice of entry; notice given within 21 days of entry shortens the appeal time to 45 days (s. 808.04(1)).","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"806.07","t":"Relief from judgment or order","s":"Grounds mirror FRCP 60(b): mistake, inadvertence, surprise or excusable neglect; newly discovered evidence; fraud or misconduct; void judgment; satisfaction; reversal of a prior judgment; prospective inequity; or any other reason. Motions within a reasonable time, and no more than one year for mistake or fraud grounds; newly-discovered-evidence motions follow s. 805.16. The motion does not affect finality.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"806.08","t":"Stay of proceedings to enforce a judgment","s":"Injunction and receivership judgments are not stayed unless ordered. The court may stay enforcement pending post-trial or relief motions on security, and may suspend or modify an injunction pending appeal. Stays pending appeal are governed by s. 808.07 (bond capped at $100 million).","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"806.09","t":"Restitution after reversal","s":"If a collected judgment is reversed, the trial court orders restitution with interest; if a new trial is ordered, the collecting party may keep the money on bond.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"806.10–806.14","t":"Judgment and lien docket; transcripts to other counties","s":"Money judgments are entered in the judgment and lien docket on payment of the fee. Covers tax warrants, transcripts of municipal court judgments, and docketing a transcript of a judgment in other counties to create a lien there.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"806.15","t":"Lien of judgment","s":"A docketed judgment is a lien for 10 years from entry on the debtor’s nonexempt real property in the county (not the exempt homestead), including after-acquired property. The period is suspended while enforcement is delayed by law, and the lien may be released when the judgment is secured on appeal.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"806.16–806.17","t":"Appellate and federal judgments","s":"Appellate judgments (e.g., for costs) are entered in the circuit court on remittitur. Federal court judgments may be docketed in the county to create a lien.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"806.18–806.22","t":"Assignment and satisfaction of judgments","s":"Assignments are filed and noted on the docket. Judgments are satisfied by an acknowledged instrument or docket entry; the court may order satisfaction. A creditor who fails to satisfy within 7 days after full payment and request owes $50 plus actual damages. Satisfied judgments are not liens; partial satisfactions are recorded.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"806.23","t":"Action on judgment","s":"No new action may be brought on a Wisconsin judgment between the same parties without leave of court for good cause on notice.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"806.24","t":"Uniform Enforcement of Foreign Judgments Act","s":"An authenticated sister-state or federal judgment filed with the clerk is treated as a Wisconsin judgment. The creditor files an affidavit with names and addresses, the clerk mails notice, and no execution may issue until 15 days after filing. Enforcement is stayed on proof of a pending appeal or stay with the required security.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"806.245","t":"Tribal court documents: full faith and credit","s":"Judgments, records and acts of Wisconsin tribal courts and legislative bodies get full faith and credit if the tribe is federally organized, documents are authenticated, the tribal court is a court of record, the judgment is valid, and the tribe reciprocates.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"806.25","t":"No judgment without action","s":"A cognovit authorization in a note to confess judgment without an action is void.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"},{"n":"806.30–806.44","t":"Uniform Foreign-Money Claims Act","s":"For claims payable in foreign currency: the parties may agree on the money of the claim; otherwise the court determines it. Judgment is stated in the foreign money, payable in that money or, at the debtor’s option, in U.S. dollars at a bank-offered spot rate on the conversion date. Covers interest, enforcement of foreign-money judgments from other states, distribution proceedings, and currency revalorizations.","u":"https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/statutes/statutes/801"}]});
